\n1. Tax Calculations: In tax calculations, precise calculations are essential for ensuring that taxes are accurately computed and reported. This involves adding and subtracting amounts and converting between different units of measurement.\n2. Example: Suppose an individual earns $50,000 per year and has deductions of $10,000. The tax rate is 20%. The individual wants to calculate the total tax owed.\n```\n  $50,000\n- $10,000\n---------\n```\nFirst, calculate the taxable income:\n```\n  $50,000\n- $10,000\n---------\n```\nStarting from the rightmost column (cents place):\n0 - 0 = 0\nNext column: 0 - 0 = 0\nNext column: 0 - 0 = 0\nNext column: 0 - 1 = -1 (we need to borrow 1 from the next column)\nNext column: 4 - 1 = 3\nSo, the taxable income is $40,000.\n\nNow, calculate the tax owed:\n```\n  $40,000\n- 20% of $40,000\n---------\n```\nFirst, calculate 20% of $40,000:\n```\n  $40,000\n- 0.20 * $40,000\n---------\n```\nStarting from the rightmost column (cents place):\n0 - 0 = 0\nNext column: 0 - 0 = 0\nNext column: 0 - 0 = 0\nNext column: 4 - 0 = 4\nSo, 20% of $40,000 is $8,000.\n\nNow, subtract the tax from the taxable income:\n```\n  $40,000\n- $8,000\n---------\n```\nStarting from the rightmost column (cents place):\n0 - 0 = 0\nNext column: 0 - 0 = 0\nNext column: 0 - 0 = 0\nNext column: 4 - 8 = -4 (we need to borrow 1 from the next column)\nNext column: 3 - 1 = 2\nSo, the total tax owed is $3,000.\n\nIn this example, understanding place value and borrowing is crucial because we need to add and subtract amounts and convert between different units of measurement. The individual needs to ensure that the calculations are accurate to report the correct tax owed.\n\nThese examples demonstrate the importance of understanding place value and borrowing in real-world scenarios related to tax calculations.\n</think>\n<answer>\nUnderstanding place value and borrowing is crucial in tax calculations, especially when dealing with precise calculations and conversions between 